Q5.What is the difference between run() and start()?\
Ans .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs64 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
1. 
\f3\fs67 start()
\f2\fs64  method\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f4\b0\fs52 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Declared in 
\f6\fs54 Thread
\f4\fs52  class.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Creates a 
\f2\b new thread of execution
\f4\b0 .\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Calls the 
\f6\fs54 run()
\f4\fs52  method 
\f2\b in a separate call stack
\f4\b0 .\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Once you call 
\f6\fs54 start()
\f4\fs52 , the JVM schedules the thread for execution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Example:
\f4\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs54 \cf0 \
class MyThread extends Thread \{\
    public void run() \{\
        System.out.println("Running in: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());\
    \}\
\}\
\
public class Test \{\
    public static void main(String[] args) \{\
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread();\
        t1.start(); // starts a new thread\
        System.out.println("Main thread: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());\
    \}\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

