Q7. What is false sharing?\
Ans.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs66 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
False sharing
\f4\b0  happens when multiple threads modify 
\f2\b different variables
\f4\b0  that happen to lie 
\f2\b close together in memory
\f4\b0  (same CPU cache line).\
Even though the variables are 
\f7\i independent
\f4\i0 , the CPU cache system treats them as one block (cache line).\uc0\u8232 This causes 
\f2\b unnecessary cache invalidations
\f4\b0  and 
\f2\b slows down performance
\f4\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs54 \cf0 Why It Happens\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f4\b0\fs66 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Modern CPUs don\'92t load variables one by one.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
They load data in chunks called 
\f2\b cache lines
\f4\b0  (usually 64 bytes).\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If two threads work on different variables that fall into the same cache line:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f5 \uc0\u9702 
\f4 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
One thread\'92s update invalidates the cache line for the other.\
\ls11\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f5 \uc0\u9702 
\f4 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
CPU keeps \'93bouncing\'94 cache lines between cores.\
\ls11\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f5 \uc0\u9702 
\f4 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
This creates contention even though there\'92s 
\f2\b no real data dependency
\f4\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

