Q11. What is N+1 select problem?\
\
Ans\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs70 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\f2\b N+1 Select Problem
\f4\b0  is a common performance issue in 
\f2\b ORM frameworks
\f4\b0  like 
\f2\b Hibernate
\f4\b0  or 
\f2\b JPA
\f4\b0  when fetching related entities from a database. It can cause 
\f2\b a large number of SQL queries
\f4\b0  and drastically slow down your application.\
Let me break it down step by step:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs50 \cf0 1. The Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs70 \cf0 Suppose you have two entities: 
\f6\fs48 Author
\f4\fs70  and 
\f6\fs48 Book
\f4\fs70 , where an 
\f6\fs48 Author
\f4\fs70  has many 
\f6\fs48 Book
\f4\fs70 s.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs48 \cf0 \
@Entity\
class Author \{\
    @Id\
    private Long id;\
    private String name;\
\
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "author")\
    private List<Book> books;\
\}\
\
@Entity\
class Book \{\
    @Id\
    private Long id;\
    private String title;\
\
    @ManyToOne\
    private Author author;\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs70 \cf0 Now, you want to fetch all authors 
\f2\b and their books
\f4\b0 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs48 \cf0 \
List<Author> authors = authorRepository.findAll();\
for (Author author : authors) \{\
    System.out.println(author.getBooks().size());\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs70 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs50 \cf0 2. What Actually Happens\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0
\f4\b0\fs70 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The ORM executes 
\f2\b 1 query
\f4\b0  to fetch all authors:\uc0\u8232 
\f6\fs48 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 SELECT * FROM author;\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f4\fs70 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Then, for each author, it executes 
\f2\b 1 query
\f4\b0  to fetch their books:\uc0\u8232 
\f6\fs48 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 SELECT * FROM book WHERE author_id = 1;\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
SELECT * FROM book WHERE author_id = 2;\
\ls23\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
SELECT * FROM book WHERE author_id = 3;\
\ls23\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
...\
\ls23\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f4\fs70 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 If you have 
\f2\b N authors
\f4\b0 , you run 
\f2\b 1 + N queries
\f4\b0 . Hence the name 
\f2\b N+1 select problem
\f4\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs50 \cf0 3. Why It's Bad\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0
\f4\b0\fs70 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If 
\f6\fs48 N
\f4\fs70  is large, it can cause 
\f2\b hundreds or thousands of queries
\f4\b0 , drastically slowing down the application.\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Wastes database resources and increases latency.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs50 \cf0 4. How to Solve It\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs70 \cf0 Solution 1: Eager Fetch / Join Fetch
\f4\b0 \uc0\u8232 Use 
\f6\fs48 JOIN FETCH
\f4\fs70  to load authors and books in 
\f2\b one query
\f4\b0 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs48 \cf0 \
@Query("SELECT a FROM Author a JOIN FETCH a.books")\
List<Author> findAllAuthorsWithBooks();\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs70 \cf0 Generates a single SQL query:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs48 \cf0 \
SELECT a.*, b.* \
FROM author a \
JOIN book b ON a.id = b.author_id;\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs70 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Solution 2: Batch Fetching
\f4\b0 \uc0\u8232 Configure the ORM to fetch collections in batches instead of one by one:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs48 \cf0 \
@BatchSize(size = 10)\
private List<Book> books;\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs126 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs70 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Key Takeaway:
\f4\b0  The N+1 select problem happens when your ORM executes 
\f2\b 1 query for the main entity + N queries for each related entity
\f4\b0 , which can be avoided with 
\f2\b join fetches, batch fetching, or projections
\f4\b0 .
\f1\b\fs84 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
